# Security Rules

These rules are non-negotiable.

## Lawful use (REA)

REA is for software you own or are authorized to examine: research, interoperability, preservation, debugging, and coordinated disclosure.

Refuse and stop if asked to:

- circumvent DRM, license checks, anti-cheat, or other technical protection measures
- develop game cheats or tamper with online services
- bypass authentication, bot protection, or access controls on unauthorized systems
- copy proprietary software or reproduce protected code and assets
- write exploits, exploit PoCs, malware, or attack procedures
- use cracked Hopper / IDA / JEB / similar, stolen keys, or license bypasses

Possession of a file, a public download, or a debug port is not authorization for every operation. Runtime attach, traffic capture, and device changes need their own permission.

## Secrets and captures

- Never commit API keys, tokens, cookies, session IDs, or crash dumps with credentials.
- HAR, SAZ, pcap, qlog, and process captures often contain secrets. Redact before quoting. Store raw files under `evidence/raw/` (gitignored).
- Never log secrets. Never paste them into reconstructions, README, or chat summaries.
- Provider license files and Hopper/IDA activation data stay off this repo.

## Reconstructions we write

- Never render raw HTML from captured pages or user input without sanitization.
- Parameterized queries only. No string-concatenated SQL.
- Auth checks for any companion app happen server-side.
- Validate at every trust boundary. Prefer allowlists.
- Do not ship captured third-party credentials, certificates, or signing keys into our code.

## Sensitive operations

- Destructive actions (delete investigation, wipe evidence, reset REA session without save) require explicit confirmation.
- Do not execute untrusted targets on the host when a safer snapshot or static path exists.
- Do not expose internal paths, home-directory dumps, or capture payloads in published notes.
